entry low-level-waste
term: Low-Level Waste (LLW)
difficulty: intermediate
image: ../../assets/images/terms/llw.webp
related_terms: radioactive-waste
related_terms: spent-fuel
related_terms: shielding
related_terms: half-life
quick_facts: ~90% of radioactive waste volume, ~1% of its radioactivity
quick_facts: Includes hospital, laboratory, and industrial items
quick_facts: Often decays to background within hours or years
quick_facts: Disposed in engineered near-surface facilities
quick_facts: Segregated and tracked from cradle to grave

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Radioactive Waste Management","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle/nuclear-wastes.aspx"}

definition:
    The everyday side of radioactive waste: gloves, gowns, filters, tools, labware, and smoke-detector sources — lightly contaminated items from hospitals, research labs, industry, and power plants. It makes up roughly 90% of radioactive waste by volume but only about 1% of its total radioactivity. Most of it decays to background within hours to years and goes into engineered near-surface disposal cells with monitored caps and drains.

debunking_snippet:
    “All nuclear waste is glowing drums destined for deep mountains.” The overwhelming majority by volume is low-level waste — contaminated paper, plastic, and filters — safely buried a few meters underground.
